# plans/plan_dx9.md D9-A5 scene corpus: dualtexture_quad
# The second non-BasicEffect Stock Effect in the corpus: DualTextureEffect (VertexColorEnabled=
# false), on a NEW custom vertex format (Position+TexCoord0+TexCoord1, stride 28 -- VSInputTx2,
# D9-82d's own new D3D9-only vertex declaration; real XNA has no built-in dual-UV vertex struct
# either, so both sides define their own IVertexType/VertexDeclaration, exactly as a real XNA game
# using DualTextureEffect would have to).
#
# texture0 = solid white (255,255,255,255), texture1 = solid (100,60,20,255), DiffuseColor=
# (0.5,0.5,0.5). Real formula (verified live against D9-82d's own DirectX9_DrawEx check): result =
# texture0 * texture1 * 2 * DiffuseColor -- the "*2*0.5" cancels out, so the expected quad color is
# exactly texture1's own color, (100,60,20,255). 1x1 textures deliberately avoid any point-filter
# tie-break ambiguity (textured_quad.scene's own concern) entirely, since there is only one texel.
width=256
height=256
profile=HiDef
clearcolor=100,149,237,255
effect=DualTextureEffect
vertexformat=PositionDualTexture
vertexcolor=false
texture=true
texturewidth=1
textureheight=1
texturefilter=Point
texture2=true
texture2width=1
texture2height=1
diffusecolor=0.5,0.5,0.5
primitive=TriangleList
texturepixel=255,255,255,255
texture2pixel=100,60,20,255
# x,y,z,u0,v0,u1,v1 -- both UV sets identical since each texture is a single texel.
vertex=-0.6,0.6,0,0,0,0,0
vertex=0.6,0.6,0,1,0,1,0
vertex=-0.6,-0.6,0,0,1,0,1
vertex=0.6,0.6,0,1,0,1,0
vertex=0.6,-0.6,0,1,1,1,1
vertex=-0.6,-0.6,0,0,1,0,1
